# Plan — etude-3xt

**Bead:** `etude-3xt` [BUG] — "the retro cadence check measures retro refs, not the
retro markdown lanes actually write"

## The defect, restated

Two artifacts claim to be "a retro":

- **the ref** — `refs/etude/retros/<id>`, written by `etude retro capture|generate`.
  Carries the manifest: scope, trigger, subjects (`subject_run.N` / `bead.N`),
  decision, the meta sidecar, provenance. Pushable, and check (d) of
  `scripts/dogfood-completeness-audit.sh` hard-fails when it is not pushed.
- **the markdown** — `.etude/retros/<name>.md`, hand-written and committed by lanes.
  Carries the prose and nothing else — no subject linkage, no trigger, no sidecar.

Nothing connects them. Check (c) counts refs with `trigger=cadence-retro`; the
practice produces markdown. On epic `etude-9uf` that produced three markdown files
and two refs. The check can therefore pass with zero retros written, or warn with
three written.

The root cause is the same one the ledger already records for this project
(`retro-ledger.md`, finding 2026-05-27, B16): *"the run-capture habit held but the
retro/gate-capture habits lapsed without any signal."* `etude retro capture` is a
second, separately-remembered step after the markdown is written, and a
separately-remembered step is the thing that lapses.

## Chosen fix

Follow the bead's preferred option — keep the ref, make the practice produce it —
and make it structural rather than procedural:

> **`etude retro capture` and `etude retro generate` land the retro body as
> markdown under `<repo-root>/.etude/retros/<retro-id>.md`, and record that path in
> the manifest as `refs["retro_file"]`.**

So the committed markdown a lane must land on `origin/main` becomes an **output of
the command that writes the ref**, not an input a second command has to be
remembered for. A lane that has the markdown has the ref, because the markdown came
out of capture. The file name *is* the ref name, so `.etude/retros/<id>.md` ⟺
`refs/etude/retros/<id>` is checkable by inspection from either side.

Two directions, two rules:

1. **`--file` outside `.etude/retros/` (or `-`, stdin):** after the ref commit
   succeeds, write the body to `<root>/.etude/retros/<retro-id>.md` (create-only —
   the id is freshly allocated, so an existing file is an error, never a clobber),
   `MkdirAll` the directory, and print `wrote .etude/retros/<retro-id>.md`.
2. **`--file` already inside `<root>/.etude/retros/`:** the markdown has already
   landed. Write nothing — record the file's existing repo-relative path in
   `retro_file` and print it. Without this branch, capturing a retro a lane wrote by
   hand would drop a second copy of the same prose in the repo under a different
   name, which is the divergence again in a new costume.

`retro_file` becomes a reserved `--ref` key (like `scope`, `trigger`,
`produced_via`), so a caller cannot forge or clobber it. `retro show` already prints
unrecognised refs under `metadata:`, so it surfaces with no display change.

Both rules live in `assembleAndWriteRetro` (`internal/cli/retro.go`), which capture
and generate already share, so this is one implementation and both paths get it.

Sequencing inside `assembleAndWriteRetro`:

- resolve the repo root and decide the target path **before** `retro.Writer.Write`,
  so a non-repo / unresolvable root fails before any ref exists;
- set `refsMap["retro_file"]` before the manifest is built (it must be inside the
  manifest that gets committed);
- write the file **after** the ref commit succeeds, so a failed ref write leaves no
  stray markdown. If the file write then fails, return an error naming both the ref
  that exists and the file that does not. That failure is loud and rare
  (permissions); silently swallowing it would recreate the exact drift this bead is
  about.

## Why not the alternatives

- **Make check (c) count markdown instead.** Cheapest, and rejected for the reason
  the bead gives: the markdown carries no subject linkage, so the check could no
  longer say *which* beads a retro covered — the cadence rule is "one retro per 3
  closed beads", which needs the subjects. It also drops the pushed-ness guarantee
  check (d) provides for refs.
- **Drop the ref, markdown only.** Deletes the manifest, sidecar, and `etude retro
  list|show` — a feature this repo exists to dogfood.
- **A new `etude retro sync`/`--all` that sweeps `.etude/retros/` for uncaptured
  markdown.** This is markdown-first, and it does *not* make divergence impossible:
  it is still a second command someone has to remember, i.e. the present bug with
  one more subcommand. It is also close in shape to the retroactive capture path
  deliberately deleted in `e0c59b6`.
- **Put the emission behind an opt-in flag (`--land` / `--write-file`).** An opt-in
  flag is precisely the step that lapses. Making it unconditional is the point.

## Scope boundary — what this bead does NOT do

Check (c) is **not touched**. It keeps counting `cadence-retro` refs and keeps
being WARN-only, exactly as `etude-9uf.4` left it. This bead removes the *cause* of
the divergence (markdown produced without a ref); re-tightening the check on the
new, now-trustworthy evidence is a separate decision and a separate commit. The
`.etude/retros/*.md` files already committed keep their existing names and are not
migrated or renamed — they are historical prose, and rewriting landed retros to
chase a naming scheme would be churn with no reader.

## Contract / behavior changes

| Surface | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| `etude retro capture` | writes a ref; stdout `captured <sha>` + `ref <ref>` | additionally lands the body under `.etude/retros/`; stdout gains a `wrote <path>` line |
| `etude retro generate` | same | same |
| retro manifest `refs` | no file linkage | adds `retro_file` = repo-relative markdown path |
| `--ref retro_file=…` | accepted as a custom ref | rejected as reserved |
| `.etude/retros/` | written only by hand | also written by the CLI (precedent: `etude init` writes `<root>/.etude`) |

Backward compatibility: existing retro refs have no `retro_file` and stay readable —
it is an additive optional key, and `retro show` renders unknown refs generically.
No manifest-version bump.

## Planned regressions

- **Planned regression:** `TestRetroCaptureLandsMarkdownUnderEtudeRetros` — capture
  with `--file retro.md` (outside the dir) writes `.etude/retros/<retro-id>.md` with
  bytes identical to the source, and the manifest records
  `refs["retro_file"] == ".etude/retros/<retro-id>.md"`. Fails today: no file.
- **Planned regression:** `TestRetroCaptureInPlaceDoesNotDuplicate` — capture with
  `--file .etude/retros/<existing>.md` leaves the directory holding exactly that one
  file, and records `refs["retro_file"]` pointing at it. Fails today (`retro_file`
  absent) and pins the no-second-copy rule.
- **Planned regression:** `TestRetroCaptureRejectsReservedRetroFileRef` —
  `--ref retro_file=x` is rejected as reserved.
- **Planned regression:** `TestRetroGenerateLandsMarkdownUnderEtudeRetros` — the
  generated body lands too, proving the shared path covers both commands.
- **Planned regression:** capture from stdin (`--file -`) lands the body under the
  allocated id, asserted inside the first test's table or as a sibling case.

`make test` and `make lint` must be green before verify is called done. Existing
capture/generate/show tests that assert stdout or `retro show` output will be
re-run and adjusted only where the new `wrote` line or `retro_file` metadata line
is genuinely expected — no assertion will be loosened to accommodate a surprise.

## Files

- `internal/cli/retro.go` — the landing rule, `retro_file`, reserved-key addition.
- `internal/cli/retro_test.go` — the regressions above.
- `docs/retro.md` — document the landed-markdown output and `retro_file` (docs phase).
